  "about": "Twenty-one solids with the bar and the bulk longitudinal speed in two separate columns, which is the reason this table is worth holding beside the others: most compilations print one speed and leave the reader to guess which. Ten of the twenty-one print a rule rather than a Poisson ratio and nine print no bar speed, and a rule is served as an absent field rather than shifted out of the way. The table's last column, the product of critical frequency and thickness, is held as printed. It is Norton & Karczub's own approximation, the speed of sound in air squared over 1.8 times the speed in the row (the bar speed where the row prints one and the bulk speed where it does not, by the footnote on the heading), and it is not the plate-speed product this library derives for rows that print none: for aluminium the page prints 12.7 m/s where the plate speed gives 11.9. One modulus is refused as a misprint. The appendix credits several sources at the foot without saying which row came from which, so no row carries an attribution.",
